6. Spring AI and LangChain4j implementation guide
Participants may choose Spring AI or LangChain4j as the primary implementation. The domain model, processor contract, tools, policies, output schema and acceptance tests must remain identical. Implementing both is useful for a controlled comparison, but it must not create two divergent applications.
6.1 Capability mapping
Capability	Spring AI	LangChain4j	Common requirement
Model orchestration	ChatClient for fluent calls; ChatModel for lower-level control.	AI Services for declarative agents; ChatLanguageModel/ChatModel for lower-level control.	Provider, credentials, timeout and temperature supplied through configuration.
Prompts	System/user messages and PromptTemplate resources.	@SystemMessage, @UserMessage and resource/message templates.	Same role, facts, guardrails, variables and output contract.
Tools	Typed methods/callbacks registered with the client; structured parameters and results.	Methods annotated with @Tool and registered with AI Services.	Allow-list, deterministic training results, timeouts, provenance and safe failure.
Structured output	Typed entity mapping or output converter, then application validation.	Typed AI Service return or explicit JSON-to-DTO mapping, then validation.	One FraudCaseRecommendation schema; reject invalid enums or missing fields.
Memory	Case-keyed chat memory through advisor/repository patterns.	Case-keyed ChatMemory; MessageWindowChatMemory and ChatMemoryStore when persistence is needed.	Bounded retention; never share context between case IDs.
RAG ingestion	Document reader/splitter, metadata and VectorStore.	Document parser/splitter, TextSegment metadata and EmbeddingStore ingestion.	Preserve document ID, version, section and chunk index.
RAG retrieval	SearchRequest plus advisor or custom retriever.	EmbeddingStoreContentRetriever and RetrievalAugmentor, or custom retrieval.	Configurable Top-K and threshold; return citations; abstain on no match.
Observability	Framework observations with Micrometer/OpenTelemetry integration.	Listeners/hooks plus Micrometer/OpenTelemetry integration.	Correlation ID, latency, token/usage metadata if available, and redaction.
Mode selection	spring-ai profile / conditional bean.	langchain4j profile / conditional bean.	Rules mode remains independent and always testable.

6.2 Prompt contract
•	Define the role as a payment-fraud and dispute triage assistant, not a final decision maker.
•	Use only validated case facts, tool results and retrieved policy passages.
•	Never fabricate evidence, policy text, tool execution or completed actions.
•	List missing or contradictory information and reduce confidence or abstain appropriately.
•	Return only the agreed schema; always set humanReviewRequired=true.
•	Provide concise evidence-linked rationale. Do not expose private model reasoning.
Variable	Origin	Purpose
caseSummary	Validated intake DTO	Customer report and transaction facts.
investigationGoal	Application constant	Keeps the task narrowly scoped.
toolEvidence	Tool results	Current structured operational evidence.
policyContext	Retriever	Approved passages and citation metadata.
conversationContext	Case memory	Follow-up context isolated by case ID.
outputSchema	Application resource	Constrains the machine-readable response.
guardrails	System prompt and configuration	Blocks unapproved actions and unsupported claims.

6.3 Spring AI adapter sketch
Representative structure; adapt to approved Spring AI version
final class SpringAiFraudCaseAgent implements FraudCaseProcessor {
  private final ChatClient chatClient;
  private final RecommendationValidator validator;

  public FraudCaseRecommendation analyze(FraudCaseContext context) {
    FraudCaseRecommendation result = chatClient.prompt()
        .system(systemPromptResource)
        .user(u -> u.text(userTemplate).params(context.promptVariables()))
        .tools(evidenceTools)
        .advisors(caseMemoryAdvisor(context.caseId()), policyAdvisor(context))
        .call()
        .entity(FraudCaseRecommendation.class);
    return validator.requireHumanReview(result);
  }
}

6.4 LangChain4j adapter sketch
Representative structure; build the AI Service with model, tools, memory and retrieval augmentation
interface FraudInvestigationService {
  @SystemMessage(fromResource = "prompts/fraud-system.txt")
  FraudCaseRecommendation analyze(
      @MemoryId String caseId,
      @UserMessage String casePrompt);
}

final class LangChain4jFraudCaseAgent implements FraudCaseProcessor {
  private final FraudInvestigationService service;
  private final RecommendationValidator validator;

  public FraudCaseRecommendation analyze(FraudCaseContext context) {
    var result = service.analyze(context.caseId(), context.renderPrompt());
    return validator.requireHumanReview(result);
  }
}

6.5 Tool implementation rules
Rule	Spring AI implementation	LangChain4j implementation
Typed contract	Register typed tool methods/callbacks with descriptions and validated parameters.	Annotate typed methods with @Tool and register tool object(s) with the AI Service.
Provenance	Return toolResultId, observedAt and origin in the result DTO.	Return the same DTO fields from @Tool methods.
Failure	Convert timeout/not-found into a structured unavailable result.	Return the same structured failure; do not throw sensitive provider details to the model.
Testing	Invoke tool methods directly and test orchestration with mocked client/model.	Invoke @Tool methods directly and test AI Service adapter with mocked model or service seam.
Safety	Expose read-only training capabilities only.	Expose the same allow-listed read-only capabilities only.

6.6 RAG pipeline
1. Validate the policy document and metadata: documentId, title, version, effectiveDate, section and owner.
2. Split by meaningful headings and token limits; do not break a critical rule across unrelated chunks.
3. Create Spring AI Document objects or LangChain4j Document/TextSegment objects with equivalent metadata.
4. Generate embeddings and store chunks in VectorStore or EmbeddingStore.
5. Build the query from the case type, evidence indicators and investigation goal.
6. Retrieve Top-K passages above the minimum score; filter by effective version where applicable.
7. Inject only relevant passages and instruct the model to abstain when context is absent.
8. Return exact citation metadata to the UI and persist it with the recommendation.
6.7 Memory and follow-up
•	Use caseId as the memory identifier in both frameworks.
•	Limit retained messages and do not store full sensitive payloads in long-lived memory.
•	Persist memory only through an approved repository/store and document restart behaviour.
•	Test that a fact introduced in Case A cannot be recalled in Case B.
•	Follow-up responses must use the same evidence and citation model; they must not bypass recommendation validation.
6.8 Framework parity tests when both are implemented
•	Run the same synthetic case set with the same model, temperature, tool fixtures, prompt intent and policy pack.
•	Assert schema validity, required evidence/citation fields and human-review flag.
•	Compare tool selection, grounded citations, abstention, latency and implementation complexity.
•	Document differences without claiming either framework is universally superior.
