8. Implementation plan and definition of done
8.1 Recommended build phases
Phase	Focus	Exit evidence
1. Foundation	Project, domain enums, validation, health endpoint, secure example configuration.	Clean build; intake validation tests; no AI dependency required.
2. Deterministic core	Persistence, audit events, evidence tools and rules processor.	Same input produces same tools/results; rules mode works with model disabled.
3. AI adapter	Implement either Spring AI or LangChain4j against FraudCaseProcessor.	Prompt contract, tool calling and validated structured recommendation work.
4. Grounding	Policy ingestion, embeddings, retrieval and citation mapping.	Relevant queries cite passages; irrelevant queries abstain.
5. Memory and UI	Case-scoped follow-up memory and six participant screens.	No cross-case leakage; full intake-to-review flow.
6. Hardening	Failure handling, injection tests, observability, accessibility and role controls.	Security and evaluation tests pass; sensitive logging absent.
7. Comparison extension	Implement the second framework against the same interfaces.	Parity report across the same approved synthetic cases.

8.2 Core definition of done
•	Application builds and starts from documented commands in a clean environment.
•	At least six synthetic cases cover different classifications, routes and evidence conditions.
•	At least four distinct evidence tools are invoked across the case suite; results are deterministic and traceable.
•	Rules mode works with the model disabled; AI mode safely handles invalid or unavailable model output.
•	RAG returns citation metadata and abstains when no relevant policy context is available.
•	Memory is isolated by case ID and tested for leakage.
•	No secrets, production data or autonomous financial/customer-impacting actions are present.
•	Human disposition and rationale are persisted and audited.
•	All six sample UI capabilities are demonstrable with accessible loading, empty and error states.
